(LST2-21, LST2-22, LST2-24 (LANGUAGE.md:2997-3009): `element N of <list>`
 is 1-indexed, works with a LITERAL index at any in-range position, and
 works with a runtime VARIABLE index reading the same slot a literal
 would. `element 1 of` is asserted every call - LST2-21's own claim is
 the 1-indexing itself, not merely that access works - while a SECOND,
 randomly positioned read alternates between the literal and the
 variable form, so 'gen leaf list inrange' no longer being the only leaf
 that reads a list by index also stops being the only leaf that reads
 index 1 every single time (its own row notes that as an unjustified
 invariant).)

To 'gen leaf list element index' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'the stem' is 'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'the name' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'the stem'.
    a number called 'the count draw' is 'rng below' of 5.
    a number called 'the count' is 'the count draw' add 2.
    a number called 'the index draw' is 'rng below' of 'the count'.
    a number called 'the chosen index' is 'the index draw' add 1.
    a number called 'the first stored value' is 0.
    a number called 'the chosen value' is 0.
    a text called 'the elements text' is "".
    a number called 'the position' is 1.
    While 'the position' is less than or equal to 'the count',
        a number called 'the value' is 'rng below' of 1000,
        If 'the position' is 1 then,
            Set 'the first stored value' to 'the value'.
        If 'the position' is 'the chosen index' then,
            Set 'the chosen value' to 'the value'.
        If 'the position' is 1 then,
            Set 'the elements text' to "{'the value'}".
        If 'the position' is greater than 1 then,
            Set 'the elements text' to "{'the elements text'}, {'the value'}".
        Set 'the position' to 'the position' add 1.
    a text called 'the declaration' is "a list called {'the name'} is [{'the elements text'}]".
    a text called 'the first subject' is "element 1 of {'the name'}".
    a text called 'the first check' is 'gen assert number is' of "LST2-21" and 'the first subject' and 'the first stored value'.
    a list called 'the lines' is ['the declaration', 'the first check'].
    a number called 'the form draw' is 'rng below' of 2.
    If 'the form draw' is 0 then,
        a text called 'the subject' is "element {'the chosen index'} of {'the name'}",
        a text called 'the check' is 'gen assert number is' of "LST2-22" and 'the subject' and 'the chosen value',
        append 'the check' to 'the lines'.
    If 'the form draw' is 1 then,
        a text called 'the index holder' is 'gen buffer companion' of "chosen position",
        a text called 'the holder declaration' is "a number called {'the index holder'} is {'the chosen index'}",
        a text called 'the subject' is "element {'the index holder'} of {'the name'}",
        a text called 'the check' is 'gen assert number is' of "LST2-24" and 'the subject' and 'the chosen value',
        append 'the holder declaration' to 'the lines',
        append 'the check' to 'the lines'.
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

( ── collections leaves, batch C, 2026-08-30 night ──

 Ten rows of docs/ledger/collections-a.md (LST-*) and collections-b.md
 (LST2-*) that hold on BOTH the installed 0.4.14 and the 0.4.15 stack -
 every 0.4.15-only claim (nested collections as copies, `Free` on a
 list/map, `element N of` returning a copy, self-append) is excluded per
 tonight's common brief and left for after the release. All ten assert;
 none only exercises.

 Two small helpers, alongside 'gen noun other than' above, for drawing
 more than one distinct map key from 'the value nouns' without a nested
 retry loop (PROCEDURE 6a: a While whose body ends on a bare nested If
 leaves the While itself open for whatever is drawn next).)

To 'gen noun other than either' with a text called 'the first word' and a text called 'the second word'.
    a text called 'a word' is 'gen word from' of 'the value nouns'.
    While 'a word' is equal to 'the first word' or 'a word' is equal to 'the second word',
        Set 'a word' to 'gen word from' of 'the value nouns'.
    Return a text, 'a word'.

To 'gen noun other than any of three' with a text called 'the first word' and a text called 'the second word' and a text called 'the third word'.
    a text called 'a word' is 'gen word from' of 'the value nouns'.
    While 'a word' is equal to 'the first word' or 'a word' is equal to 'the second word' or 'a word' is equal to 'the third word',
        Set 'a word' to 'gen word from' of 'the value nouns'.
    Return a text, 'a word'.

(LST-43, LST-45 (LANGUAGE.md:2548, 2555-2558): `Set map's "key" to value`
 both INSERTS a new entry and REPLACES an existing one - a replace
 leaves `length` unchanged, an insert grows it by exactly one. `gen leaf
 map inrange` only ever replaces its own one starting key; no leaf
 before this one ever inserted a second key into a map.

 A map-key read (`m's "key"`) cannot be handed straight to 'gen assert
 number is' as its subject: the read's own text already contains a
 literal `"`, and interpolating that inside an ASSERT's quoted message
 would need the escaping doubled. Captured into a plain number holder
 first - the same shape 'gen assert text is' uses for buffer content -
 the holder is then a bare identifier, safe anywhere.)

To 'gen leaf map insert and replace' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'the stem' is 'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'the name' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'the stem'.
    a text called 'the length access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and "length".
    a text called 'the starting key' is 'gen word from' of 'the value nouns'.
    a text called 'the inserted key' is 'gen noun other than' of 'the starting key'.
    a number called 'the starting value' is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a number called 'the replaced value' is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a number called 'the inserted value' is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a text called 'the declaration' is "a map called {'the name'} is {{\"{'the starting key'}\": {'the starting value'}}}".
    (A map-key access built off 'the name' (the CLOSED reference form)
     would double its own closing quote when 's is appended after it -
     'the message slip''s, not 'the message slip's. 'gen buffer
     property' builds the possessive off 'the stem' instead, the same
     way it does for a plain property; the quoted key is just handed to
     it as the "property" text.)
    a text called 'the starting key quoted' is "\"{'the starting key'}\"".
    a text called 'the starting key access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and 'the starting key quoted'.
    a text called 'the inserted key quoted' is "\"{'the inserted key'}\"".
    a text called 'the inserted key access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and 'the inserted key quoted'.
    a text called 'the replace line' is "Set {'the starting key access'} to {'the replaced value'}".
    a text called 'the replace holder' is 'gen buffer companion' of "replaced value seen".
    a text called 'the replace capture' is "a number called {'the replace holder'} is {'the starting key access'}".
    a text called 'the replace value check' is 'gen assert number is' of "LST-45" and 'the replace holder' and 'the replaced value'.
    a text called 'the replace length check' is 'gen assert number is' of "LST-45" and 'the length access' and 1.
    a text called 'the insert line' is "Set {'the inserted key access'} to {'the inserted value'}".
