(LST-49 (LANGUAGE.md:2570-2581): a missing key does not crash - it sets
 the error flag - and the value handed to the destination SPLITS: where
 the compiler can PROVE the key absent from a literal map it can see
 whole, the read is the untyped NUMBER 0 regardless of destination
 type; where it cannot (a map grown through `Set`), the read yields the
 destination's own TYPED default instead - checked here on a text, a
 list and a map destination, none of which any leaf tried before.
 `gen leaf map oob` catches the error but never captures or checks what
 came back.

 Same shape as `gen leaf absent key` (VAL-27, values.md) - a map, a
 missing key, a stale-flag drain before the first risky read - but a
 different claim: VAL-27 is "is nothing" versus "raises an error"; this
 is the provable/unprovable SPLIT and what each destination type
 actually receives. Reuses that leaf's naming idiom ('gen fresh noun' /
 'gen written name' / 'gen possessive name') rather than 'gen buffer
 name': a possessive built from an already-quoted companion name would
 double its closing quote (see 'gen buffer property's own comment), and
 the possessive is needed here on four different holders.)

