(LST2-94 (LANGUAGE.md:3300-3302): a `treating X as Y` clause on a loop
 expansion replaces the loop variable with Y for exactly the iterations
 where it equals X - `gen leaf treating print`/`treating grid` draw the
 match and the collection from the same range, so a hit is possible but
 never guaranteed. Here the match is PLANTED at a random position, so
 the substitution is certain to fire at least once; the expected value
 at every position is computed from the ORIGINAL element, not from the
 planted position, so an accidental second collision (the source drew
 the match value again by chance elsewhere) is asserted correctly too.
 `append each ... treating ... to` is the sink, since `print each` gives
 no way to capture what the loop actually did.)

