( ── collections leaves, batch D, 2026-08-30 night ──

 docs/ledger/collections-a.md LST-52, LST-55, LST-60 (folds LST-61),
 LST-62, LST-63, LST-64, LST-65, LST-66, LST-67, LST-69 - ten rows in
 the Maps and Type Predicates sections (LANGUAGE.md:2590-2687), the
 240-259 reservation's last span (249-258; 240-248 are batch C's).
 Every 0.4.15-only claim (nested collections as copies, `Free` on a
 list/map, `element N of` returning a copy, self-append) is excluded per
 tonight's common brief and left for after the release - none of these
 ten touch that ground, since Type Predicates and the map-value-nested
 claim both predate it.

 Six of the ten reuse the runtime-tag vocabulary values.md already built
 for the `value` ABI ('gen tag predicate', 'gen bare tag name', 'gen
 value payload', 'gen value expression', 'gen taggable other than',
 further down this file) rather than inventing a second one - the same
 six/seven tags, the same predicate nouns, just applied to a plain list
 element instead of a `value`-typed one.

 Hand-reduced while building this batch: a `For each` whose single-line
 body contains its own `If` needs TWO closing periods where more program
 text follows, not one - the first closes the If (PROCEDURE 6a's "one
 period closes one level"), and the For-each itself is a second, separate
 level. 'gen tag list has' below already carries this (`Set 'the tag
 appears' to true..`); it just was not yet written down as a load-bearing
 reason to avoid For-each+If in a LEAF's own emitted lines. Every leaf
 here sidesteps the question entirely by asserting on `element N of` a
 list rather than iterating it with `For each`, which is why LST-60's
 worked-example dispatch chain is proven by direct per-position checks,
 not by reproducing the manual's own loop.)

