(A blank line BEFORE a function definition, not just after it.

 LANGUAGE.md:83: inside an open clause a blank line is not cosmetic - it
 force-closes every clause still open. LANGUAGE.md:91: a `To` is legal
 only at the top level, and one reached while some other clause is still
 open is a compile error, not an absorption.

 Both matter here because a leaf is not the only thing on the page. A
 leaf that emits a multi-line `For each` whose last line closes only its
 inner `If` leaves the loop clause open and relies on the NEXT
 statement's period to close it - which works for every statement except
 a `To`. `gen leaf cast and break` (src/gen_misc.vox, kind 35) is exactly
 that shape, so a value leaf drawn straight after it failed to compile:
 8 programs in a 220-seed campaign, every one of them
 "A function is defined at the top level, like a thing".

 The blank line closes whatever was left open, so a definition-bearing
 leaf is safe wherever it is drawn. The underlying shape in kind 35 is a
 separate surface's defect and is in the batch report for the master
 rather than fixed here.)

To 'gen open a function definition'.
    'gen emit' of "\n".

(VAL-02 (LANGUAGE.md:2685-2687): the three declaration forms - the
 parameter `with a value called x`, the return `Return a value, <expr>`,
 and the local `a value called r` - emitted in one program, with a value
 carried through all three and asserted on the way out.

 'gen leaf value return' already puts all three forms on the page, but
 always in one shape: its return hands back the parameter verbatim and
 its local is always initialised from the call. Neither is a rule the
 manual writes. Here the return hands back either the parameter or a
 literal of its own, and the local appears in all three of ITS shapes -
 initialised from a literal, initialised from the call, and (the
 manual's own wording, `a value called r`) with no initialiser at all.

 The uninitialised local is EMITTED and never ASSERTED. The manual does
 not say what a `value` declared without an initialiser holds, so an
 assertion would encode this generator's reading of an open question
 rather than the manual's claim. What it actually reports is in the
 batch report, for the master to adjudicate.)

