(VAL-12 (LANGUAGE.md:2777-2779): after a successful in-place retype the
 variable is tracked with the NEW type for the rest of its lifetime, and
 retyping to the type it already holds is a no-op.

 Two claims, two halves, both in one leaf because the second is only
 meaningful beside the first.

 The tracking half is asserted three ways, because "tracked as the new
 type" has three observable consequences and a compiler could get any
 one of them right while losing the others: the new type's predicate
 answers true, the OLD type's predicate answers false, and - where the
 new type is a number - arithmetic on the variable works and gives the
 sum the generator computed. Arithmetic is the operation that is valid
 ONLY after the retype: on an untouched `value` it is a compile error
 (VAL-08, LANGUAGE.md:2735), so its succeeding here is the tracking
 working.

 The no-op half asserts the payload is unchanged, the tag is unchanged,
 and no error flag was raised - the last through a drained handler, so a
 flag left standing by an earlier leaf cannot masquerade as this one's.)

To 'gen leaf value retype tracking' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    (0 number, 1 text, 2 float, 3 boolean - the four retype targets
     LANGUAGE.md:2746 names. The source is written as the text spelling
     of the answer, which is the manual's own worked shape and the only
     one where the generator knows the converted result exactly.)
    a number called 'the target' is 'rng below' of 4.
    a text called 'the source payload' is 'gen retype source' of 'the target'.
    a text called 'the phrase' is 'gen fresh noun'.
    a text called 'the name' is 'gen written name' of 'the phrase'.
    a text called 'the possessive' is 'gen possessive name' of 'the phrase'.
    a text called 'the target predicate' is 'gen tag predicate' of 'the target'.
    a text called 'the target tag name' is 'gen tag name' of 'the target'.
    a text called 'the target type text' is 'gen dynamic type text' of 'the target'.
    a text called 'the tag it reported' is 'gen type phrase' of 'the possessive'.
    (A text target starts from a number so the conversion has something
     to do; the other three start from the text spelling of the answer,
     so the tag it starts under is a text.)
    a number called 'the source tag' is 1.
    a text called 'the source literal' is "\"{'the source payload'}\"".
    If 'the target' is 1 then,
        Set 'the source tag' to 0,
        Set 'the source literal' to 'the source payload'.
    a text called 'the source predicate' is 'gen tag predicate' of 'the source tag'.
    a text called 'the source bare tag name' is 'gen bare tag name' of 'the source tag'.
    'gen emit' of "{indent}a value called {'the name'} is {'the source literal'}.\n".
    'gen emit' of "{indent}{'the name'} is {'the target predicate'}.\n".
    a text called 'the new tag assertion' is "{indent}If {'the name'} is not {'the target predicate'} then, Print \"ASSERT VAL-12: expected {'the target tag name'} after the retype got {'the tag it reported'}\", Exit 95.\n".
    'gen emit' of 'the new tag assertion'.
    a text called 'the old tag assertion' is "{indent}If {'the name'} is {'the source predicate'} then, Print \"ASSERT VAL-12: expected the {'the source bare tag name'} tag to be gone got {'the tag it reported'}\", Exit 95.\n".
    'gen emit' of 'the old tag assertion'.
    a text called 'the type assertion' is "{indent}If {'the possessive'} type is not \"{'the target type text'}\" then, Print \"ASSERT VAL-12: expected {'the target type text'} got {'the tag it reported'}\", Exit 95.\n".
    'gen emit' of 'the type assertion'.
    (The operation that is valid only for the new type. A number target
     is the one whose result the generator can compute, so it is the one
     that carries the arithmetic; the other three make the tracking
     claim through the two predicates above.)
    If 'the target' is 0 then,
        a number called 'the numeral' is 'the source payload' as a number,
        a number called addend is 'rng below' of 1000 add 1,
        a number called 'the sum' is 'the numeral' add addend,
        a text called 'the arithmetic assertion' is "{indent}If {'the name'} add {addend} is not {'the sum'} then, Print \"ASSERT VAL-12: expected {'the sum'} got {{{'the name'} add {addend}}}\", Exit 95.\n",
        'gen emit' of 'the arithmetic assertion'.
    (The no-op half: retype it again to the type it now holds.)
    a text called 'the witness phrase' is 'gen fresh noun'.
    a text called 'the witness name' is 'gen written name' of 'the witness phrase'.
    'gen emit' of "{indent}a number called {'the witness name'} is 0.\n".
    'gen drain the error flag' of indent and 'the witness name'.
    'gen emit' of "{indent}{'the name'} is {'the target predicate'}.\n".
    'gen emit' of "{indent}On error Set {'the witness name'} to 1.\n".
    a text called 'the quiet assertion' is "{indent}If {'the witness name'} is not 0 then, Print \"ASSERT VAL-12: expected no error retyping to {'the target tag name'} it already holds got {{{'the witness name'}}}\", Exit 95.\n".
    'gen emit' of 'the quiet assertion'.
    a text called 'the unchanged tag assertion' is "{indent}If {'the possessive'} type is not \"{'the target type text'}\" then, Print \"ASSERT VAL-12: expected {'the target type text'} after the no-op got {'the tag it reported'}\", Exit 95.\n".
    'gen emit' of 'the unchanged tag assertion'.
    (The payload after the no-op. A float's payload interpolates as its
     bit pattern rather than its digits (this file's own note), so the
     comparison is written for every target but the reported value is
     the tag where the payload cannot be shown.)
    a text called 'the comparison' is 'the source payload'.
    If 'the target' is 1 then,
        Set 'the comparison' to "\"{'the source payload'}\"".
    a text called 'what it holds' is 'gen got phrase' of 'the name' and 'the possessive' and 'the target'.
    a text called 'the payload assertion' is "{indent}If {'the name'} is not {'the comparison'} then, Print \"ASSERT VAL-12: expected {'the source payload'} after the no-op got {'what it holds'}\", Exit 95.\n".
    'gen emit' of 'the payload assertion'.
    'gen emit' of "{indent}Print {'the possessive'} type.\n".

(VAL-14 (LANGUAGE.md:2793-2800): the `type` property reads the runtime
 tag and returns a text description with a `(dynamic)` suffix - `Text
 (dynamic)`, `Number (dynamic)`, `Float (dynamic)`, `Boolean (dynamic)`,
 `List (dynamic)`, `Map (dynamic)`, `Nothing (dynamic)`. The manual
 (LANGUAGE.md:2804) says those seven are the whole list, so a tag
 outside them is itself a finding.

 The generator declares the value, so it knows the tag, so it asserts
 the exact string rather than printing it. Which tags a given emission
 covers is NOT pinned by the row - the row asks for the property, not
 for a fixed roll call - so the count and the tags are drawn, and a
 campaign covers all seven many times over. Emitting the same seven in
 the same order every time would put a fixed vocabulary of seven lines
 into every program that draws this leaf, which is precisely the
 undeclared rule CLAUDE.md forbids.)

