(VAL-25 (LANGUAGE.md:2871-2877): `is nothing` and `is not nothing` are
 the test, and they are an EQUALITY like `is true`, not a type
 predicate - there is no `is a nothing`.

 Both spellings are asserted in both directions, on an absent value and
 on a present one, which is four assertions and needs all four: a
 runtime that answered `is nothing` for everything would pass two of
 them, and one that answered it for nothing would pass the other two.

 The two directions that must be TRUE are asserted through a witness
 rather than an `Otherwise` branch, so every line closes the clause it
 opens - an assertion that ended on `Otherwise, ...` would leave the
 else branch continuing into whatever the generator emitted next
 (gen_buffers.vox's 'gen assert number' has the full account).)

