    'gen emit' of 'the present denial'.
    'gen emit' of "{indent}Print {'the absent witness name'}.\n".

(VAL-27 (LANGUAGE.md:2905-2909): reading a map key that was never set is
 an ERROR, not `nothing` - the read raises the error flag rather than
 silently handing back an absent value, so "the key is absent" and "the
 key holds nothing" stay distinguishable.

 What this leaf must NOT do is assert the missing read equals `nothing`:
 that it does not is the entire claim. The assertion is on the flag, via
 `On error`, and the contrast entry - a key that IS present and holds
 `nothing` - is asserted the other way: it reads back as nothing and
 raises no flag at all.

 Both handlers sit behind a drained one, so a flag left standing by an
 earlier leaf cannot make the absent read look caught, nor the present
 read look guilty.

 The absent key comes from 'the absent keys', a vocabulary kept disjoint
 from the one the present keys are drawn from - the row's claim is only
 tested if the key really is missing, and a key drawn from the same
 words could land on one that was written.)

To 'gen leaf absent key' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'the map phrase' is 'gen fresh noun'.
    a text called 'the map name' is 'gen written name' of 'the map phrase'.
    a text called 'the map possessive' is 'gen possessive name' of 'the map phrase'.
    a text called 'the present key' is 'gen word from' of 'the value nouns'.
    a text called 'the missing key' is 'gen word from' of 'the absent keys'.
    (How many entries stand beside the one that holds nothing. Zero is
     reachable and legal - a map whose only entry is the absent value -
     and nothing says a map must be bigger than that.)
    a number called 'how many extra entries' is 'rng below' of 3.
    a text called 'the absent spelling' is 'gen value payload' of 6.
    a text called 'the entries' is "\"{'the present key'}\": {'the absent spelling'}".
    a number called 'entries built' is 0.
    While 'entries built' is less than 'how many extra entries',
        a text called 'another key' is 'gen noun other than' of 'the present key',
        a text called 'another value' is 'gen scalar element',
        Set 'the entries' to "{'the entries'}, \"{'another key'}\": {'another value'}",
        Set 'entries built' to 'entries built' add 1.
    'gen emit' of "{indent}a map called {'the map name'} is {{{'the entries'}}}.\n".
    (The receiving type of the missing read is drawn: the manual's own
     example reads into a `number`, but the error is the READ's, not the
     receiver's, and nothing pins which type is asked to take it.

     `a text` is NOT among them, and this is the one place a rule really
     does pin the shape. The compiler resolves a never-set key at COMPILE
     time - it knows the literal key is not in the literal map - and
     rejects `a text called <x> is <map>'s "<missing>"` outright, saying
     the read yields the number 0. So a text receiver is a known compile
     error, and the generator's contract is legal Vox that should compile
     AND run (CLAUDE.md); emitting one would be a generator defect, not a
     finding. The compile-time half of this behaviour is undocumented -
     LANGUAGE.md:2905 describes the missing read as a runtime error only -
     and is in the batch report as a candidate for the master.)
    a number called 'the receiver choice' is 'rng below' of 2.
    a text called 'the receiver type' is "a number".
    If 'the receiver choice' is 1 then,
        Set 'the receiver type' to "a value".
    a text called 'the reading phrase' is 'gen fresh noun'.
    a text called 'the reading name' is 'gen written name' of 'the reading phrase'.
    a text called 'the witness phrase' is 'gen fresh noun'.
    a text called 'the witness name' is 'gen written name' of 'the witness phrase'.
    'gen emit' of "{indent}a number called {'the witness name'} is 0.\n".
    'gen drain the error flag' of indent and 'the witness name'.
    'gen emit' of "{indent}{'the receiver type'} called {'the reading name'} is {'the map possessive'} \"{'the missing key'}\".\n".
    'gen emit' of "{indent}On error Set {'the witness name'} to 1.\n".
    a text called 'the flag assertion' is "{indent}If {'the witness name'} is not 1 then, Print \"ASSERT VAL-27: expected reading the never-set key {'the missing key'} to raise the error flag got {{{'the witness name'}}}\", Exit 95.\n".
    'gen emit' of 'the flag assertion'.
    (The contrast: the key that IS there and holds nothing reads back as
     nothing, quietly.)
    a text called 'the entry phrase' is 'gen fresh noun'.
    a text called 'the entry name' is 'gen written name' of 'the entry phrase'.
    a text called 'the entry possessive' is 'gen possessive name' of 'the entry phrase'.
    a text called 'what the entry reported' is 'gen type phrase' of 'the entry possessive'.
    a text called 'the second witness phrase' is 'gen fresh noun'.
    a text called 'the second witness name' is 'gen written name' of 'the second witness phrase'.
    'gen emit' of "{indent}a number called {'the second witness name'} is 0.\n".
    'gen drain the error flag' of indent and 'the second witness name'.
    'gen emit' of "{indent}a value called {'the entry name'} is {'the map possessive'} \"{'the present key'}\".\n".
    'gen emit' of "{indent}On error Set {'the second witness name'} to 1.\n".
    a text called 'the quiet assertion' is "{indent}If {'the second witness name'} is not 0 then, Print \"ASSERT VAL-27: expected reading the present key {'the present key'} to raise no flag got {{{'the second witness name'}}}\", Exit 95.\n".
    'gen emit' of 'the quiet assertion'.
    a text called 'the present assertion' is "{indent}If {'the entry name'} is not nothing then, Print \"ASSERT VAL-27: expected nothing at the present key {'the present key'} got {'what the entry reported'}\", Exit 95.\n".
    'gen emit' of 'the present assertion'.
    'gen emit' of "{indent}Print {'the witness name'}.\n".
